[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. SIGPIPE disposition changed the exit status (fixed)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** With stdout connected to a pipe that had no reader, the C program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m was killed by `SIGPIPE` (signal 13, shell status 141) while the Rust program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exited 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust standard library sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runs. The C program does nothing to `SIGPIPE`, so it uses whatever disposition
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m it inherited across `exec`. Under `SIG_DFL` the failing `write` from stdout's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exit-time flush kills the C process; the Rust process instead saw `EPIPE`, which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the translation ignores (as does the C code, which never checks `printf`'s
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m return value), and exited 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **First attempt, which was wrong.** Unconditionally calling
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL)` at the top of `main`. This over-corrected: the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m disposition C uses is the *inherited* one, and it is not always `SIG_DFL`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `SIG_IGN` is inherited across `exec`, so when the parent ignores `SIGPIPE` --
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m which is the case for any parent that is itself a Rust program, including
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `cargo test`'s own harness -- the C program inherits `SIG_IGN` and exits 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Forcing `SIG_DFL` made the Rust program die with signal 13 where the C program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exited 0, i.e. the same mismatch with the sign flipped.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `translation/src/main.rs` now records the inherited disposition in an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `.init_array` constructor, which the loader runs before `main` and therefore
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m before the standard library's initialisation replaces it, and restores that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m recorded value as the first action in `main`. The translation then matches the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program under either inherited setting.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Verified.** `readerless_stdout_with_default_sigpipe` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `readerless_stdout_with_ignored_sigpipe` set the disposition in the forked child
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m before `exec`, so both binaries start from an identical inherited value.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Independently confirmed outside the Rust harness: under `SIG_DFL` both processes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m report signal 13, under `SIG_IGN` both report exit 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Test-harness defect found while verifying (not a translation mismatch)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The first version of the readerless-stdout test used `Stdio::piped()` for stdout
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and dropped the read end after spawning. It passed serially but failed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m intermittently under `cargo test`'s default parallelism, reporting the C program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m as exiting 0. Cause: the read-end descriptor was transiently held open by a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m child process spawned concurrently by another test, so the C program's write
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m into the pipe buffer succeeded and no `SIGPIPE` was raised. The test now uses a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m per-invocation FIFO whose read end is opened and closed by the test process
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m alone; `open` sets `O_CLOEXEC`, so no other child can inherit it. Stable over
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m repeated parallel runs of the whole suite in both debug and release profiles.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
